• Parameter: A configurable internal variable within a model that is learned from training data to define its behavior.
• Temperature: A hyperparameter that controls the randomness or creativity of an AI's generated responses.
• Gen AI: Artificial intelligence systems capable of creating new content like text, images, or audio based on patterns learned from training data.
• Token: A basic unit of text (like a word or a syllable) that an AI model processes and generates.
• RAG: Retrieval-Augmented Generation, a technique that improves AI accuracy by fetching relevant facts from external sources before generating a response.
• Agentic AI: Systems designed to autonomously plan, use tools, and take sequential actions to achieve complex, long-term goals.
• Bias: Systematic errors or prejudices in an AI's outputs resulting from skewed or unrepresentative training data.
• Embedding: A mathematical vector representation that captures the semantic meaning and relationships of data or text.
• Fine Tune: The process of taking a pre-trained model and training it further on a specific dataset to adapt it for a specialized task.
• Hallucination: A phenomenon where an AI confidently generates false, inaccurate, or fabricated information.
• Context Window: The maximum amount of text (measured in tokens) that an AI model can process or remember at a single time.
• Vector DB: A specialized database designed to efficiently store, index, and search high-dimensional vector embeddings based on semantic similarity.
• Schema: A structured blueprint or framework that defines how data is organized, formatted, or categorized within a system.
• Prompt: The input text, instruction, or question provided to an AI model to guide its generated response.
• MCP: Model Context Protocol, an open standard that enables AI models to securely connect and share context with external tools and data sources.
• LangChain: A popular open-source software framework designed to simplify the creation of applications using large language models.
• Skills: Predefined capabilities or functions that an AI model can call upon to execute specific tasks.
• Neural Network which can learn: An interconnected computational structure inspired by the human brain that adjusts its internal pathways to recognize patterns from data.
• Agents: Autonomous AI systems engineered to perceive their environment, make decisions, and execute tasks independently.
• Weights: The numeric values within a neural network that determine the strength and influence of a connection between processing nodes.
• Chatbot: A software application designed to simulate human conversation through text or voice interactions.
• Attention Number: Not valid (This is not a standard industry term; you likely mean the Attention Mechanism, which computes numeric weights to determine which parts of an input sequence a model should focus on).
• Harness: A testing framework or suite of software tools used to benchmark, evaluate, and stress-test the performance of AI models.
• Prompt Injection: A security vulnerability where malicious user inputs trick an AI into ignoring its original instructions and executing unauthorized commands.
• GuardRails: Safety mechanisms and programmatic boundaries implemented around an AI model to filter out toxic, biased, or unwanted outputs.
• Parding: Not valid (This is a typo; you likely mean Parsing, which is the process of analyzing a string of data or text to break it down into structured, recognizable components).
• Evals: Systematic evaluations and benchmarks used to measure an AI model's accuracy, safety, and functional performance.
• Chunks: Smaller, manageable pieces of a larger dataset or document created to fit within an AI model's processing limits.
• Ground Truth: The foundational, verified data used as the absolute standard of accuracy to train and evaluate AI models.
Invalid / Typo Alerts:
• Attention Number: Not a standard term; typically referred to as the Attention Mechanism or Attention Weights.
• Parding: Not a valid AI term; likely a typo for Parsing.
